1 @

   Runs a command procedure: a file of DCL commands.

   Format

     @filespec [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

2 Parameters

 filespec

   The command procedure, a file on a mounted volume or on the host, by
   the same rules as the other commands' file names.  The file type is
   .COM unless one is given.  A host file name with lowercase letters or
   a "/" must be quoted.

 p1 ... p8

   Up to eight parameters, separated by blanks.  The procedure sees them
   as the local symbols P1 to P8; those not given are "".  Letters are
   made uppercase unless quoted; quotes keep blanks and case, and ""
   passes an empty parameter.

2 Description

   The procedure runs at a new command level, one deeper than the level
   that ran it.  The terminal is command level 0, and procedures may
   nest 32 levels deep.

   In the procedure, each command line starts with "$", and "$!" starts
   a comment line.  A line ending in "-" continues on the next line,
   which has no "$".  A line without a "$" is data, for whatever reads
   the procedure's input (SYS$INPUT): an image the procedure runs (by
   LIB$GET_INPUT, RMS's $GET, or a terminal $QIO read), TYPE SYS$INPUT,
   READ SYS$INPUT, the debugger once a DEBUG or RUN/DEBUG in the
   procedure has started it, or the interactive assembler.  An image
   reading the data comes to its end of file at the next "$" line; DECK
   lets data lines start with "$".  Data that nothing reads is skipped
   after an image, with a SKPDAT warning, up to the next "$" line (a lone
   "$" will do); after one of DCL's own commands (an assignment, IF, SET
   VERIFY, ...), a data line is read as a command.

   Each command level has its own local symbols (assigned with "=" or
   ":="), which go when the procedure ends; global symbols ("==" and
   ":==") stay.  A procedure sees the local symbols of the levels that
   ran it.

   The procedure ends at its last line, at an EXIT, or when a command
   fails with an error or a fatal error (a warning doesn't end it).  An
   error ends the procedures that ran it too.  ON and SET [NO]ON change
   what a failure does, and EXIT can give the procedure's status (see
   ON, EXIT, and Symbol_Assign Status).

2 Qualifiers
/OUTPUT=filespec

   Sends the procedure's output to the file rather than the terminal
   (.LIS by default; NL: discards it).  Error messages go to the
   terminal too.  A file on a volume is made when the procedure starts,
   and written a line at a time.  The qualifier must follow the file
   name with no blank between them; otherwise it is a parameter.

2 Examples

   1.$ @SETUP

     Runs SETUP.COM in the default directory.

   2.$ @"setup.com" "Test Run" fast

     Runs the host file setup.com with P1 "Test Run" and P2 "FAST".

   3.$ @NIGHTLY/OUTPUT=NIGHTLY.LOG

     Runs NIGHTLY.COM, its output written to NIGHTLY.LOG.
